l user-item interactions, with A𝑖𝑗= 1 when user 𝑖already\nhas clicked (or other pre-defined behaviors) item 𝑗. B ∈R𝑁×𝑁\nwhich is a one-dimensional least-squares curve fitting problem of\nsize 𝑁. In the following section, we will try to find out the properties\nof 𝑓(·) by observing the relationship between the eigenvalues and\nthe diagonal of the transformed test interactions.\n3.1.2\nObservations of Real World Dataset. The relationship\nbetween the diagonals of Λ and U𝑇BU on four real-world datasets\nare shown in Figure 1. Due to the large scale of datasets that causes\na long time for eigendecomposition, BFS starting from some ran-\ndomly selected nodes is conducted to reduce the size of user-item\ngraphs. From the figure we have the following discoveries:\nis the adjacency matrix of missing user-item interactions where\nB𝑖𝑗= 1 indicates that user 𝑖will have an interaction with item\n𝑗. Usually, in industrial scenarios, interactions of B are regarded\nto occur later than those of A. Then the recommendation model\ncan be considered as a mapping 𝑓(·) from the existing interactions\nto the future possible ones. Given 𝑓(·) a matrix polynomial, the\noptimization of the transformation 𝑓(·) can be formulated as [29]:\nminimize\n𝑓∈H\n||𝑓(A) −B||𝐹\n(7)\nwhere H is the hypothesis space of all possible transformations\nand || · ||𝐹is the Frobenius norm. As A is a real symmetric matrix,\nthrough the eigendecomposition, we have A = UΛU𝑇where U ∈\nR𝑁×𝑁is a group of orthogonal eigenvectors and Λ ∈R𝑁×𝑁is a\ndiagonal matrix with all corresponding eigenvalues on the diagonal.\nGiven the assumption that 𝑓is a matrix polynomial, Eq (7) can be\nrewrite as:\n(1) The low-frequency component (values near 1.0) and high-\nfrequency component (values near -1.0) are positively cor-\nrelated to the diagonal of U𝑇BU, indicating that we can\npreserve information of these two components by relatively\nsimple models so that patterns from historical interactions\ncan be easily promoted to the unseen ones.\n(2) The mid-frequency component (values around 0.0) of the\ntraining graph is less correlated with signals on the test\ngraph, meaning that signals within this band contain less\ninformation about the user interest.\n||𝑓(A) −B||𝐹= ||𝑓(UΛU𝑇) −B||𝐹\n(8)\n= ||U𝑓(Λ)U𝑇−B||𝐹\n= ||𝑓(Λ) −U𝑇BU||𝐹.\nEq (8) is derived based on the fact that the Frobenius norm is invari-\nant with the multiplication of an orthogonal matrix on the right and\nits transpose on the left. Equation 8 can be decomposed into two\nparts: the Frobenius norm of off-diagonal entries in 𝑓(Λ) −UBU𝑇\nthat is independent of 𝑓, and the Frobenius norm of its diagonal\nentries. Then it leads to the following equivalent objective:\n∑︁\n\u0010\n𝑓(Λ𝑖𝑖) −U𝑇\n·𝑖BU·𝑖\n\u00112\n,\n(9)\nminimize\n𝑓∈H\n3.1.3\nInspiration for the Filter Design. From the above obser-\nvations, we can conclude some key properties a graph filter should\nhave for better recommendations. The filter should behave like a\nband-stop filter that preserves the low-frequency component and\nthe high-frequency component, as they are linearly correlated with\nthe future possible interactions and are easy to fit. At the same\ntime, as the correlations of middle-frequency are quite intricate, the\nfilter should either suppress such signals or use some non-linear\nfunctions to transform the middle-frequency signal to the target\nspectrum, i.e., diag(U𝑇BU). Besides in Figure 1, we find the filer\nshould have positive responses at the low-frequency area, while\nhaving negative responses at the high-frequency area.\n𝑖--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMESSAGE RECOVERY ATTACK TO NTRU USING A LATTICE (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.09620)\nciteturn9686search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; When we write LF for some polynomial F ∈R, we mean a subset ... is called a lattice and the ﬁnite vector set B = {b1, . . . , bn} is called a basis of the ... We denote by M the n × m matrix having as rows the vectors b1, . . . , bn.\nMESSAGE RECOVERY ATTACK TO NTRU\n3\n1.1.3. NTRU-HPS cryptosystem. Alice chooses public parameters (N, p, q, d) with\nN and p primes, gcd(N, q) = gcd(p, q) = 1. To get an idea of the size and form of\nthe parameters, say p is small (usually 3) and N, q are large (of the same order)\nand q is a power of 2. The degree parameter N is chosen prime due to the attack\nof Gentry [18] when N is composite. What is more, a prime integer N maximizes\nthe probability an element of Rq be invertible in the case where q is a prime power.\nAlso, we deﬁne the set of ternary polynomials T (d1, d2) ⊂R, be the polynomials\nof R with d1 entries equal to one, d2 entries equal to minus one, and the remaining\nentries are zero. When we write LF for some polynomial F ∈R, we mean a subset\nof T (d1, d2) for some d1, d2, relatively small with respect to q. Alice chooses her\nprivate key (f(x), g(x)), such that, f(x) ∈Lf and g(x) ∈Lg 5, where f(x) is\ninvertible in Rq and Rp. If f(x) is invertible, then the inverses are easily computed\nin Rp and Rq by using Euclidean algorithm and Hensel’s Lemma. Let Fq(x) and\nFp(x) be the inverses of f(x) in Rq and Rp, respectively. Alice next computes\nh(x) = Fq(x) ⋆g(x)\nmod q.\nThe polynomial h(x) is Alice’s public key. The problem of distinguishing h(x) from\nuniform elements in Rq is called decision NTRU problem. Whilst, the problem of\nﬁnding the private key (f(x), g(x)) is called, search NTRU problem.\nBob’s plaintext is a polynomial m(x) ∈R whose coeﬃcients are integers in the\ninterval [−1\n2(p −1), 1\n2(p −1)]. In other words, the plaintext m(x) is the centerlift of\na polynomial6 in Rp. Thus, if p = 3, then the message is the centerlift of a ternary\npolynomial.\nBob chooses a random ephemeral key7 r(x) ∈Lr (in the original\nNTRU-HPS, Lr = T (d, d)) and computes\n(1.2)\ne(x) ≡pr(x) ⋆h(x) + m(x) mod q.\nFinally, Bob sends to Alice the ciphertext e(x) ∈Rq.\nTo decrypt, Alice computes\na(x) ≡f(x) ⋆e(x) mod q.\nThen, she center